.TH MARANTZ 1 "October 24, 2026" "marantz" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
marantz \- control compatible Marantz devices over TCP
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B marantz
.RB [ \-\-debug ]
.I command
.RI [ arguments ...]
.br
.B marantz
.BR \-h | \-\-help | help
.SH DESCRIPTION
Sends the shared Denon/Marantz ASCII control protocol over raw TCP,
normally port 23. Each protocol command opens its own connection, sends a
single carriage-return-terminated command, receives a matching response when
available, and closes. Higher-level operations use sequential connections.
There is no persistent session between invocations.
.PP
Implemented with Bash and a small Python 3 standard-library transport.
No Homebrew packages, interactive telnet automation, or GUI are required.
.PP
MODEL 40n is the only device physically tested by the developer. Other models
are documented candidates or experimental.
Simulations do not certify hardware compatibility.
Supports macOS (Intel/Apple Silicon) and Linux (AMD64/ARM64), with Bash 3.2+
and Python 3.9+. Ubuntu 24.04 and Debian 12 have container verification;
other Linux distributions remain unverified. Windows is unsupported.
No netcat variant or GNU timeout utility is required.
.PP
Developed and tested on Marantz MODEL 40n. Compatibility with other Marantz
devices is experimental or unverified unless explicitly documented.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B discover
Perform fresh SSDP discovery and print candidates and addresses,
with model and friendly name when metadata is available. Ignores explicit host
selection and cached results for this search. No state changes or cache writes.
Alias: devices.
.TP
.B identify
Print discovered identity or Unknown, address and queried power state.
Explicit hosts are not assigned a model without metadata.
Alias: info.
.TP
.B capabilities
Probe the four read-only queries independently. Valid replies are Observed;
missing or malformed replies remain Unknown, not Unsupported. Control support
stays Unknown because setters are not probed. Exit 0 if any query was observed,
otherwise 1. Observations are not persisted or model-wide certification.
.TP
.B status
Query power, volume, mute and input on four separate connections and print a
summary. No state changes.
.TP
.BR "power" " [status]"
Query power and print On or Standby.
.TP
.B power on
Send PWON, poll PW? until On, and print On.
.TP
.BR "power off" " | " "power standby"
Send PWSTANDBY, poll PW? until Standby, and print Standby.
.TP
.BR "volume" " [status]"
Query MV? and print an integer or documented half-step encoded level.
The compact query has no space. This is not universal dB or percentage.
.TP
.BR "volume up" " | " "volume down"
Ensure power is on, send MVUP or MVDOWN, then query and print the new volume.
.TP
.BI "volume set " LEVEL
Ensure power is on, send an integer volume command, then query and print volume.
Only decimal integers are accepted for settings. Values below ten use MV07.
This padding and all state-changing behavior require manual device verification.
Half-step setting is not supported; query MV795 is displayed as 79.5.
.TP
.BI "volume " LEVEL
Shorthand for volume set LEVEL.
.TP
.BR vol " | " v
Aliases for volume; all volume arguments are accepted.
.TP
.BR "mute" " [status]"
Query MU? and print On or Off.
.TP
.BR "mute on" " | " "mute off"
Send MUON or MUOFF, query MU?, and print the resulting state.
.TP
.B mute toggle
Query MU?, send the opposite mute state, query again, and print the result.
.TP
.BR "input" " [status]"
Query SI? and print the source token, such as NET.
Source setting is unavailable.
.TP
.B reboot
Query power, enter standby, confirm standby, wait the configured delay, power
on, and confirm On. Prints progress. Stops on a failure; it does not continue
to power on if standby cannot be confirmed.
.TP
.BI "raw " COMMAND
Send one nonempty, literal printable ASCII command, at most 1024 bytes, followed
by CR. Print the first complete response frame and disconnect. Quote COMMAND
in the calling shell. Embedded CR, LF and control characters are rejected.
The utility never executes the argument locally. A command without a response
fails after the response timeout, although its device action may have occurred.
.TP
.B \-\-debug
Place before COMMAND to print transmitted commands and accepted response frames
to standard error. Standard output retains the normal concise result.
.TP
.BR \-h " | " \-\-help " | " help
Display full help without opening a network connection.
.SH POWER READINESS
Volume changes first require a valid MV? reply, then query PW?. If Standby,
send PWON once, wait the configurable delay, then poll until On. Polling checks
power state, not audio or network-subsystem readiness. State-changing commands
are never retried after ambiguous failures. Read-only power polling can retry
transport failures within its deadline.
.PP
Power and mute writes also require their corresponding query. A valid query
does not prove setter availability. Explicit mute and absolute-volume changes
fail if state confirmation disagrees. No setter is automatically replayed.
.SH DEVICE DISCOVERY
Normal commands require no setup files. Use an explicit MARANTZ_HOST from the
environment first. Otherwise use the validated last-known device from the
disposable cache. If the cache is missing or unavailable, perform automatic
SSDP discovery and cache a successfully discovered device for future commands.
An explicit host failure never switches to a different receiver.
.PP
SSDP uses UDP multicast 239.255.255.250:1900 with search target
urn:schemas-denon-com:device:ACT-Denon:1 and standard upnp:rootdevice.
Both use MX: 1.
At most three pairs per active IPv4 interface fit within one collection
deadline,
including sends. Interface enumeration uses OS addresses, with no fixed names,
up to 32 interfaces. An unusable interface does not prevent trying others.
Broad root responses need Marantz/Denon manufacturer metadata.
UPnP description metadata identifies devices. HTTP metadata requests are bounded
and restricted to the response sender IP, without redirects or proxies.
No full subnet port scan is performed. OS multicast routing is the fallback if
interface enumeration is unavailable. Allow LAN multicast and unicast
SSDP replies.
.PP
No model is silently preferred. Multiple candidates require MARANTZ_HOST or an
exact MARANTZ_DEVICE name, model, UDN or address. Duplicate matches fail.
A sole selected candidate must answer PW?; discovery alone is not compatibility.
.PP
Use a .local hostname exactly like an IP; macOS resolves it through Bonjour.
Linux needs working mDNS/NSS configuration, often Avahi, or use an IP address.
No specific hostname is assumed. Automatic discovery does not
parse a long-running dns-sd process or browse guessed Bonjour service types.
.SH CACHE
Successfully discovered and protocol-validated addresses with UPnP identity
metadata are saved in ~/Library/Caches/marantz/device.json on macOS, or
${XDG_CACHE_HOME:-$HOME/.cache}/marantz/device.json on Linux.
Empty, relative or control-containing XDG paths fall back to $HOME/.cache.
Missing or invalid HOME falls back to the OS account home. macOS ignores XDG.
No configuration file or ~/.config directory is required.
Each use checks age,
refetches identity metadata and probes PW? within a bounded validation deadline.
Unreachable, changed, corrupt, expired or port-mismatched entries trigger fresh
discovery. If an expired entry exists and multicast fails or returns nothing,
it can be reused only after fresh HTTP identity and TCP validation both pass.
This supports VPN multicast failures, never masks ambiguity or changed identity,
and is not cryptographic authentication. Reuse does not extend the timestamp.
Without identity metadata the address is not cached. Cache write failures are
non-fatal. The cache is disposable state, not a settings file. Missing cache
directories are recreated automatically. Deleting the cache is safe and simply
causes rediscovery on the next normal command. No manual cache management is
required. The discover command performs a fresh diagnostic search.
Uninstall preserves the cache and other user files.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B XDG_CACHE_HOME
Linux-only cache base, an absolute path; default $HOME/.cache.
Ignored on macOS. Cache writes are best-effort.
.TP
.B MARANTZ_HOST
Explicit IP address or hostname, including .local. Default: unset (automatic).
.TP
.B MARANTZ_DEVICE
Exact discovery name, model, UDN or address ignoring case; default unset.
Constrains cache reuse. MARANTZ_HOST takes precedence. Duplicate matches fail.
.TP
.B MARANTZ_DISCOVERY_TIMEOUT
Seconds for SSDP responses and a separate overall metadata budget. Default: 3.
.TP
.B MARANTZ_VERIFY_TIMEOUT
Seconds for total cached identity/protocol validation, or a discovered-device
PW? probe. Default: 1.
.TP
.B MARANTZ_CACHE_TTL
Soft freshness, integer seconds 0 through 3600. Default: 300.
Zero disables cache reads/writes. Expired fallback requires fresh validation.
.TP
.B MARANTZ_PORT
TCP port, 1 through 65535. Default: 23.
.TP
.B MARANTZ_CONNECT_TIMEOUT
Deadline for resolution and connection establishment, in seconds. Default: 2.
.TP
.B MARANTZ_RESPONSE_TIMEOUT
Deadline for sending and receiving a response, in seconds. Default: 2.
A setter with no acknowledgement is followed by an independent state query.
.TP
.B MARANTZ_POWER_TIMEOUT
Maximum seconds per power-state polling phase, integer 1 through 300.
Default: 20. Initial queries, setters, stabilization and reboot delay are
separately bounded. Reboot has two polling phases.
.TP
.B MARANTZ_POLL_INTERVAL
Seconds between power polls. Default: 0.5.
.TP
.B MARANTZ_STABILIZE_DELAY
Seconds after any PWON before the next command. Default: 4, covering an older
documented SR/NR requirement. Newer families may need less; owner verification
is required before reducing it. This does not prove audio readiness.
.TP
.B MARANTZ_REBOOT_DELAY
Seconds between confirmed standby and power on during reboot. Default: 2.
.TP
.BR MARANTZ_VOLUME_MIN " and " MARANTZ_VOLUME_MAX
Integer setting policy, default 0 through 98, configurable within 0 through 99.
This is a conservative two-digit CLI policy, not a verified MODEL 40n hardware
range. This policy applies to absolute settings, not relative or raw commands.
Query output is not constrained by it. Half-step replies are accepted; minimum
codes and dB offsets remain model-specific.
.PP
Decimal timeouts and poll interval must be 0.01 through 300 seconds; delays
may also be zero. No network operation waits forever, even if bytes trickle in.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
marantz discover
marantz identify
marantz capabilities
marantz status
marantz power
marantz v up
marantz volume set 10
marantz mute toggle
marantz input
marantz reboot
marantz raw 'MV?'
marantz \-\-debug status
MARANTZ_HOST=192.0.2.10 marantz status
MARANTZ_HOST=some-device.local marantz status
.fi
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success, including help.
.TP
.B 1
Environment, dependency, network, response or readiness failure.
Also discovery failure, ambiguity, or no devices found.
.TP
.B 2
Invalid CLI command, argument or volume level.
.SH WARNINGS
Reboot means standby to on. It is not a factory reset and may not restart
the network subsystem, which can stay active in standby. No reset or
reset-to-defaults command is implemented.
.PP
Raw mode is an expert escape hatch and transmits the supplied bytes. Use only
known safe protocol commands; the tool does not assign semantics to arbitrary
raw commands. Never send reset or destructive configuration payloads.
.PP
TCP control may be unauthenticated and is unencrypted. Never expose it publicly.
A connection
failure after sending can leave device state changed. Check status before
manually repeating a relative change or toggle. Only the reported PWON,
PWSTANDBY, MV10, MUON, MUOFF and SINET query formats are device-verified.
.SH FILES
.I ~/Library/Caches/marantz/device.json
.br
.I ${XDG_CACHE_HOME:-$HOME/.cache}/marantz/device.json
.br
.I /usr/local/bin/marantz
.br
.I /usr/local/libexec/marantz-transport.py
.br
.I /usr/local/libexec/marantz-discovery.py
.br
.I /usr/local/share/man/man1/marantz.1
.SH INSTALLATION
On macOS and Linux, run install.sh with optional --prefix and an absolute path.
Default: /usr/local. A user-local prefix such as $HOME/.local needs no sudo.
Add PREFIX/bin to PATH and use man -M PREFIX/share/man marantz if necessary.
Repeated installation atomically replaces each of the four known files;
uninstall.sh with the same prefix removes only those files and retains
the cache.
The scripts do not install dependencies or invoke sudo automatically.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR bash (1),
.BR python3 (1)
